WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:10.670
This work is about 3D printing flexible molds
for objects with detailed geometry.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:15.780
The main observation is that the final object
not only has to be cast, but also has to be

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.490
removed conveniently from the mold.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.200
Finding an appropriate layout for the cuts
is a non-trivial problem.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.950
The technique endeavors to have the least
amount of cuts, and the length of the cuts

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:28.800
is also subject to minimization.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.370
I see the light bulb lighting up in the heads
of our seasoned Fellow Scholars, immediately

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.809
noticing that this sounds like an optimization
problem.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:43.289
And in this problem, we start out from a dense
cut layout, and iteratively remove as many

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:47.739
of these cuts as possible until some prescribed
threshold is met.

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:52.850
However, we have to be vigilant about the
fact that these cuts will result in deformations

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.960
during the removal process.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.089
We mentioned before that we're interested
in shapes that have geometry that is rich

00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:03.760
in details, therefore this distortion effect
is to be minimized aggressively.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.580
Also, we cannot remove these cuts indefinitely,
because sometimes, more cuts have to be added

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.220
to reduce the stress induced by the removal
process.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:19.220
This is a cunning plan, however, a plan that
only works if we can predict where and how

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.320
these deformations will happen, therefore
we have to simulate this process on our computer.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.610
During removal, forces are applied to the
mold, which we also have to take into consideration.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:35.530
To this end, there is an actual simulation
of the entirety of the extraction process

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:40.890
to make sure that the material can be removed
from the mold in a non-destructive manner.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:41.890
Wow!

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:47.420
The paper discusses tons of issues that arise
from this problem formulation, for instance,

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.550
what one should do with the tiny air bubbles
stuck in the resin.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:57.310
Or, the optimization part is also non-trivial,
to which a highly effective homebrew solution

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.490
is presented.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.950
And there's a lot more, make sure to have
a look!

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.520
Of course, as always, we would love to hear
your ideas about possible applications of

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.560
this technique.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.480
Leave your thoughts in the comments section!

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.550
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

